    {
      "id": 225209,
      "name": "Studoku",
      "username": "Studoku",
      "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/s/ec9cab/{size}.png",
      "created_at": "2019-01-29T15:41:01.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"MindWanderer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/mindwanderer/48/4816_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> MindWanderer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>From what we’ve seen on the livestreams, it’s exactly the opposite of “labyrinthine.”&nbsp; It’s very linear and holds your hand the whole way.&nbsp; I’m not sure how much it gives up in the way of flexibility, though–Christopher’s players all used the random approach and just went wherever the system took them, and Paul’s players were talking over each other the whole time and it was hard to follow.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>As far as I could tell, the guided approach limits players to about 3 options when they make a decision. It seems good for players new to the system, or to roleplay in general, since fewer options make choices quicker and easier. Restrictions like that can also help with creativity.</p>\n<p>The free approach is apparently the same creation, but choosing the dice rolls. Way more flexibility, but potentially overwhelming. It seems like a good system for players with a character concept in mind (which probably includes everyone who’s been following the game this much).</p>",
      "post_number": 6,
      "post_type": 1,
      "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:58:47.687Z",
      "reply_count": 0,
      "reply_to_post_number": null,
